WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.310
Game of Thrones is an epic fantasy packed
with details, and that means some of those

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:07.040
details are bound to leave viewers puzzled
now and then.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.560
Well, you don't need to find a maester to
get your answers, because here's a spoiler-filled

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.400
explanation of the most confusing Game of
Thrones moments.

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.960
It's never really been clear exactly what
Bran's powers are as the Three-Eyed Raven.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.470
We know he can see events in the past, and
that he can warg into animals - and sometimes

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.670
people! - to control them and see current
events.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.560
In the finale, though, he also seems to imply
he can see the future.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:31.460
"Will you lead the Seven Kingdoms to the best
of your abilities, from this day until your

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.360
last day?"

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:34.640
"Why do you think I came all this way?"

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.780
This isn't the first time, though.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:41.280
Back in season 7, he gave Littlefinger's valyrian
dagger to Arya - which she needed to kill

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.280
the Night King.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:46.480
In fact, way back in season three, Bran's
friend and mentor Jojen Reed explained that

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.940
greenseers like them can in fact see the future.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.520
"The Raven brings the sight."

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.200
"Seeing things that haven't happened yet?"

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.280
So how much has Bran known about his future,
and for how long?

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.640
It's impossible to say - just like everything
else to do with Bran.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.360
"How will we know the end?"

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:07.760
"You'll know."

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.760
Game of Thrones was filled with prophecies,
from Daenerys seeing the show's ending in

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.420
a vision to Cersei having the deaths of her
children foretold by a witch.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:17.780
"Gold will be their crowns.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.380
Gold, their shrouds."

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.180
And then there was Melisandre's assertion
that the resurrected hero Azor Ahai would

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:32.990
end the great darkness with his sword Lightbringer.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.760
That one didn't come true.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:35.500
Or did it?

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:40.200
According to lore, Azor Ahai forged Lightbringer
by stabbing his lover in the heart - just

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.600
as Jon did with Daenerys.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.740
Like Melisandre, we all assumed the prophecy
was literal.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.780
But if it was just a metaphor, then maybe
it did happen after all.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:50.300
The Wall was the shield that guarded the realms
of men.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.860
And then, at the end of season 7, it was destroyed
by the Night King's undead dragon, allowing

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:56.340
the dead to march into Westeros.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.920
And getting a dragon was a lucky break for
the Night King, too, otherwise he might have

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.320
been stuck banging his head against that wall
forever.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:06.480
That's because the Wall was made using ancient
magic that prevented White Walkers from crossing

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:06.980
it.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.900
"The Wall's not just ice and stone.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.500
Ancient spells were carved into its foundations."

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.520
"While it stands, the dead cannot pass."

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.140
Only the power of a supernatural creature
like the dragon could break those spells and

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.320
bring the series to its inevitable conclusion.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.580
At the summit in the dragon pit at King's
Landing, the Lords and Ladies of Westeros

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.550
do more than just choose a new king in Bran.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:34.180
They also create the Six Kingdoms of Westeros,
because Sansa just… nopes out.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.360
"The North will remain an independent kingdom.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.120
As it was for thousands of years."

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.820
So why didn't anyone else do that?

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.020
Dorne has a long history of semi-autonomy,
but given the massive political upheaval there

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.770
with the murders of two consecutive rulers
and their heirs, it makes sense for this random

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:51.780
new guy to make alliances where he can.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.010
Yara and the Iron Islands, on the other hand,
had already negotiated a measure of autonomy

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.220
with Queen Daenerys.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.340
"What if everyone starts demanding their independence?"

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.580
"She's not demanding, she's asking."

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:07.720
So there's no clear reason why she would agree,
especially given her enmity towards the Starks.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.090
Our guess is that with the Iron Fleet destroyed,
House Greyjoy is simply too weak to risk going

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.440
alone - for now, that is.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.340
Seems like Bran is going to have a lot of
work ahead of him to keep this coalition together.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.840
When Jon was sent to join the Night's Watch
at the end of the final episode, even he had

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.120
to ask one pressing question:

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:26.780
"There's still a Night's Watch?!"

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.680
With the Night King destroyed, the Wall in
ruins, and the Wildlings at peace with the

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.100
North, there doesn't seem to be any purpose
to the Night's Watch.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.740
So it's fitting that Jon spends all of five
seconds there before heading even further

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.680
north with Tormund and the Wildlings.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.560
What their plans are is unknown, but the way
Tormund seems to defer to him suggests very

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.520
strongly that Jon may take his new place among
the Free Folk as King Beyond the Wall.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:48.920
Not a bad ending after all.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:54.000
The reunion of Sansa and Arya Stark at Winterfell
was one of the most emotional moments of season

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.100
7, but it was quickly replaced by tension
between the sisters.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.280
For the entire season, the two characters
were pitted against each other by Littlefinger.

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.150
And then, suddenly, in the last episode Arya
and Sansa were best friends again, working

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:06.340
together to take him down.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:09.740
Most fans were left to assume that they had
done the obvious thing and just asked Bran,

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.990
the living Wikipedia, what was really going
on.

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:15.510
The show never spelled it out, though…or
rather, they just never showed it to us.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.510
In fact, Isaac Hempstead-Wright, who plays
Bran, revealed in an interview that they actually

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.600
did film a scene where Sansa asked Bran for
the truth and he uncovered Littlefinger's

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.100
treachery.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.160
The scene never aired, but if you were wondering
how and why the sisters suddenly teamed up,

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:28.820
now you know.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.640
After Queen Daenerys is slain, her right hand
man Grey Worm demands justice, leading to

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.480
Jon's exile.

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.660
And then Grey Worm takes the Unsullied to
Naath.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:38.760
"We sail for the Isle of Naath."

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.590
If you felt like you missed something here,
well…you did.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:47.421
While the show has never actually shown Naath,
just four episodes earlier, Grey Worm and

00:04:47.421 --> 00:04:51.500
his doomed lover Missandei discussed retiring
to the tropical beaches of her homeland.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:52.460
"Naath.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:55.640
I'd like to see the beaches again."

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:00.360
"Then I will take you there."

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:05.320
We're not sure how the islanders will react
to being visited by an army of silent eunuchs,

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.910
but here's hoping Grey Worm somehow finds
a happy ending - which would be a Game of

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:09.580
Thrones first.

